# Every platform checks out the same bytes.
#
# slates has doc-truth tests that compare a record's bytes against a table generated from the
# registry it documents: crates/wire/src/observe.rs and crates/ipc/src/protocol.rs over
# docs/wip/observability.md, crates/conformance/tests/matrix.rs over docs/wip/conformance.md, and
# xtask over SLATES_DESIGN.md and README.md. Those comparisons only mean something if the file on
# disk is the file that was committed.
#
# Without this declaration they are not. GitHub's Windows runner images ship git with
# core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows checkout rewrites every LF to CRLF. The marker search
# `find("<!-- chokepoints:begin -->\n")` then cannot match a line ending `-->\r\n`, and the
# byte-for-byte table compare behind it could not match either. That is how the aarch64 Windows
# lane went red on 2026-10-20 reporting "the record has the chokepoints:begin marker" about a
# marker sitting on line 12 of the file
# (docs/bugs/2026-10-20-a-windows-checkout-rewrote-the-bytes-a-doc-truth-test-compares.md).
#
# Declaring the tree's text as LF makes the checkout deterministic on every platform — the same
# rule the determinism gates already hold the build to — and fixes the site that went red along
# with the eight siblings that read a document, rather than teaching each reader to strip carriage
# returns and leaving the tenth to rediscover it.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# vendor/ holds upstream's own files (mantle's patched aws-lc-rs and aws-lc-sys copies). `!text`
# and `!eol` *unspecify* both attributes rather than setting them, which restores exactly the state
# vendor was in before this file existed: no opinion from slates, upstream's own nested
# .gitattributes the only one that speaks.
#
# This has to be unspecify and not `-text`. vendor/aws-lc-rs/third_party/NIST/.gitattributes says
# `*.rsp eol=crlf` and says why: "Preserve the line endings on the NIST test vectors so that we can
# use sha256sum and friends to verify that they haven't been tampered with." With `-text` here, a
# `git add --renormalize .` still rewrote 263 lines in each of 24 of those vectors — every line, so
# every checksum. Those are NIST's bytes and a tampering check reads them; slates does not have an
# opinion about them to express.
vendor/**       !text !eol

# The binaries slates does own. text=auto detects these by content anyway; declaring them is so a
# reader of this file knows which tracked files are not text, and so detection is never the thing
# standing between a golden vector and its bytes.
*.png           -text
*.bin           -text
